1
00:00:04,246 --> 00:00:07,246
What keeps us healthy and happy

2
00:00:07,246 --> 00:00:08,829
as we go through life?

3
00:00:09,996 --> 00:00:12,496
If you were going to invest now

4
00:00:12,538 --> 00:00:14,579
in your future best self,

5
00:00:14,621 --> 00:00:17,579
where would you put your time
and your energy?

6
00:00:18,621 --> 00:00:21,038
There was a recent survey of millennials

7
00:00:21,038 --> 00:00:26,246
asking them what their
most important life goals were,

8
00:00:26,246 --> 00:00:28,288
and over 80 percent said

9
00:00:28,288 --> 00:00:32,413
that a major life goal for them
was to get rich.

10
00:00:32,454 --> 00:00:36,787
And another 50 percent
of those same young adults
And another 50 percent
of those same young adults

11
00:00:36,829 --> 00:00:39,371
said that another major life goal

12
00:00:39,371 --> 00:00:41,204
was to become famous.

13
00:00:42,454 --> 00:00:43,662
(Laughter)

14
00:00:43,704 --> 00:00:50,371
And we're constantly told
to lean in to work, to push harder

15
00:00:50,371 --> 00:00:52,412
and achieve more.

16
00:00:52,454 --> 00:00:56,121
We're given the impression that these
are the things that we need to go after

17
00:00:56,121 --> 00:00:57,954
in order to have a good life.

18
00:00:57,996 --> 00:01:00,204
Pictures of entire lives,

19
00:01:00,204 --> 00:01:05,412
of the choices that people make
and how those choices work out for them,

20
00:01:05,454 --> 00:01:08,329
those pictures
are almost impossible to get.
those pictures
are almost impossible to get.

21
00:01:09,579 --> 00:01:12,621
Most of what we know about human life

22
00:01:12,663 --> 00:01:16,121
we know from asking people
to remember the past,

23
00:01:16,121 --> 00:01:20,913
and as we know, hindsight
is anything but 20/20.

24
00:01:20,954 --> 00:01:24,621
We forget vast amounts
of what happens to us in life,

25
00:01:24,663 --> 00:01:27,538
and sometimes memory
is downright creative.

26
00:01:28,288 --> 00:01:32,663
But what if we could watch entire lives

27
00:01:32,704 --> 00:01:35,538
as they unfold through time?

28
00:01:35,579 --> 00:01:39,538
What if we could study people
from the time that they were teenagers
What if we could study people
from the time that they were teenagers

29
00:01:39,579 --> 00:01:42,329
all the way into old age

30
00:01:42,329 --> 00:01:45,704
to see what really keeps people
happy and healthy?

31
00:01:47,038 --> 00:01:48,246
We did that.

32
00:01:49,121 --> 00:01:51,371
The Harvard Study of Adult Development

33
00:01:51,371 --> 00:01:56,121
may be the longest study
of adult life that's ever been done.

34
00:01:57,204 --> 00:02:03,329
For 75 years, we've tracked
the lives of 724 men,

35
00:02:04,871 --> 00:02:09,371
year after year, asking about their work,
their home lives, their health,
year after year, asking about their work,
their home lives, their health,

36
00:02:09,371 --> 00:02:13,746
and of course asking all along the way
without knowing how their life stories

37
00:02:13,788 --> 00:02:15,204
were going to turn out.

38
00:02:16,788 --> 00:02:20,413
Studies like this are exceedingly rare.

39
00:02:20,413 --> 00:02:24,454
Almost all projects of this kind
fall apart within a decade

40
00:02:24,496 --> 00:02:27,663
because too many people
drop out of the study,

41
00:02:27,704 --> 00:02:30,579
or funding for the research dries up,

42
00:02:30,621 --> 00:02:32,871
or the researchers get distracted,

43
00:02:32,913 --> 00:02:36,996
or they die, and nobody moves the ball
further down the field.

44
00:02:37,788 --> 00:02:40,038
But through a combination of luck
But through a combination of luck

45
00:02:40,038 --> 00:02:43,746
and the persistence
of several generations of researchers,

46
00:02:43,788 --> 00:02:45,329
this study has survived.

47
00:02:45,996 --> 00:02:50,496
About 60 of our original 724 men

48
00:02:50,538 --> 00:02:51,829
are still alive,

49
00:02:51,871 --> 00:02:54,038
still participating in the study,

50
00:02:54,038 --> 00:02:56,079
most of them in their 90s.

51
00:02:57,038 --> 00:02:58,954
And we are now beginning to study

52
00:02:58,996 --> 00:03:02,329
the more than 2,000 children of these men.

53
00:03:03,163 --> 00:03:05,496
And I'm the fourth director of the study.

54
00:03:06,913 --> 00:03:11,621
Since 1938, we've tracked the lives
of two groups of men.
Since 1938, we've tracked the lives
of two groups of men.

55
00:03:11,663 --> 00:03:13,788
The first group started in the study

56
00:03:13,829 --> 00:03:16,496
when they were sophomores
at Harvard College.

57
00:03:16,538 --> 00:03:19,371
They all finished college
during World War II,

58
00:03:19,371 --> 00:03:21,829
and then most went off
to serve in the war.

59
00:03:22,788 --> 00:03:24,913
And the second group that we've followed

60
00:03:24,954 --> 00:03:29,121
was a group of boys
from Boston's poorest neighborhoods,

61
00:03:29,121 --> 00:03:31,163
boys who were chosen for the study

62
00:03:31,163 --> 00:03:34,496
specifically because they were
from some of the most troubled

63
00:03:34,538 --> 00:03:36,413
and disadvantaged families

64
00:03:36,413 --> 00:03:39,163
in the Boston of the 1930s.

65
00:03:39,163 --> 00:03:43,788
Most lived in tenements,
many without hot and cold running water.
Most lived in tenements,
many without hot and cold running water.

66
00:03:45,996 --> 00:03:47,913
When they entered the study,

67
00:03:47,954 --> 00:03:50,871
all of these teenagers were interviewed.

68
00:03:50,913 --> 00:03:53,121
They were given medical exams.

69
00:03:53,121 --> 00:03:56,663
We went to their homes
and we interviewed their parents.

70
00:03:56,704 --> 00:03:59,079
And then these teenagers
grew up into adults

71
00:03:59,079 --> 00:04:01,496
who entered all walks of life.

72
00:04:01,538 --> 00:04:07,621
They became factory workers and lawyers
and bricklayers and doctors,

73
00:04:11,663 --> 00:04:15,913
one President of the United States.
Some developed alcoholism.
A few developed schizophrenia.

74
00:04:16,829 --> 00:04:19,121
Some climbed the social ladder

75
00:04:19,121 --> 00:04:22,371
from the bottom
all the way to the very top,

76
00:04:22,371 --> 00:04:25,663
and some made that journey
in the opposite direction.

77
00:04:26,996 --> 00:04:29,954
The founders of this study

78
00:04:29,996 --> 00:04:31,996
would never in their wildest dreams

79
00:04:31,996 --> 00:04:36,538
have imagined that I would be
standing here today, 75 years later,

80
00:04:36,579 --> 00:04:39,663
telling you that
the study still continues.

81
00:04:40,788 --> 00:04:44,413
Every two years, our patient
and dedicated research staff
Every two years, our patient
and dedicated research staff

82
00:04:44,413 --> 00:04:47,454
calls up our men
and asks them if we can send them

83
00:04:47,496 --> 00:04:50,621
yet one more set of questions
about their lives.

84
00:04:51,538 --> 00:04:55,121
Many of the inner city Boston men ask us,

85
00:04:55,121 --> 00:04:58,996
"Why do you keep wanting to study me?
My life just isn't that interesting."

86
00:05:00,079 --> 00:05:02,454
The Harvard men never ask that question.

87
00:05:02,496 --> 00:05:07,704
(Laughter)

88
00:05:12,413 --> 00:05:15,288
To get the clearest picture
of these lives,
To get the clearest picture
of these lives,

89
00:05:15,288 --> 00:05:18,246
we don't just send them questionnaires.

90
00:05:18,246 --> 00:05:20,704
We interview them in their living rooms.

91
00:05:20,746 --> 00:05:23,663
We get their medical records
from their doctors.

92
00:05:23,704 --> 00:05:26,204
We draw their blood, we scan their brains,

93
00:05:26,204 --> 00:05:27,913
we talk to their children.

94
00:05:27,954 --> 00:05:33,204
We videotape them talking with their wives
about their deepest concerns.

95
00:05:33,204 --> 00:05:36,746
And when, about a decade ago,
we finally asked the wives

96
00:05:36,788 --> 00:05:39,163
if they would join us
as members of the study,

97
00:05:39,163 --> 00:05:41,871
many of the women said,
"You know, it's about time."

98
00:05:41,913 --> 00:05:42,954
(Laughter)

99
00:05:42,996 --> 00:05:44,663
So what have we learned?
So what have we learned?

100
00:05:44,704 --> 00:05:49,913
What are the lessons that come
from the tens of thousands of pages

101
00:05:49,954 --> 00:05:52,996
of information that we've generated

102
00:05:52,996 --> 00:05:54,204
on these lives?

103
00:05:55,204 --> 00:06:00,829
Well, the lessons aren't about wealth
or fame or working harder and harder.

104
00:06:01,996 --> 00:06:08,329
The clearest message that we get
from this 75-year study is this:

105
00:06:08,329 --> 00:06:13,538
Good relationships keep us
happier and healthier. Period.

106
00:06:14,496 --> 00:06:18,329
We've learned three big lessons
about relationships.
We've learned three big lessons
about relationships.

107
00:06:18,329 --> 00:06:22,413
The first is that social connections
are really good for us,

108
00:06:22,454 --> 00:06:24,954
and that loneliness kills.

109
00:06:24,996 --> 00:06:28,621
It turns out that people
who are more socially connected

110
00:06:28,663 --> 00:06:31,746
to family, to friends, to community,

111
00:06:31,788 --> 00:06:36,454
are happier, they're physically healthier,
and they live longer

112
00:06:36,496 --> 00:06:39,871
than people who are less well connected.

113
00:06:39,913 --> 00:06:43,329
And the experience of loneliness
turns out to be toxic.

114
00:06:43,329 --> 00:06:48,454
People who are more isolated
than they want to be from others
People who are more isolated
than they want to be from others

115
00:06:48,496 --> 00:06:51,704
find that they are less happy,

116
00:06:51,746 --> 00:06:54,663
their health declines earlier in midlife,

117
00:06:54,704 --> 00:06:56,913
their brain functioning declines sooner

118
00:06:56,954 --> 00:07:00,496
and they live shorter lives
than people who are not lonely.

119
00:07:01,538 --> 00:07:04,746
And the sad fact
is that at any given time,

120
00:07:04,788 --> 00:07:09,371
more than one in five Americans
will report that they're lonely.

121
00:07:10,538 --> 00:07:13,204
And we know that you
can be lonely in a crowd

122
00:07:13,204 --> 00:07:15,871
and you can be lonely in a marriage,

123
00:07:15,913 --> 00:07:18,038
so the second big lesson that we learned
so the second big lesson that we learned

124
00:07:18,038 --> 00:07:21,163
is that it's not just
the number of friends you have,

125
00:07:21,163 --> 00:07:24,663
and it's not whether or not
you're in a committed relationship,

126
00:07:24,704 --> 00:07:29,329
but it's the quality
of your close relationships that matters.

127
00:07:30,038 --> 00:07:34,829
It turns out that living in the midst
of conflict is really bad for our health.

128
00:07:34,871 --> 00:07:38,829
High-conflict marriages, for example,
without much affection,

129
00:07:38,871 --> 00:07:44,621
turn out to be very bad for our health,
perhaps worse than getting divorced.

130
00:07:44,663 --> 00:07:49,413
And living in the midst of good,
warm relationships is protective.
And living in the midst of good,
warm relationships is protective.

131
00:07:49,454 --> 00:07:52,538
Once we had followed our men
all the way into their 80s,

132
00:07:52,579 --> 00:07:55,579
we wanted to look back at them at midlife

133
00:07:55,621 --> 00:07:57,204
and to see if we could predict

134
00:07:57,204 --> 00:08:01,204
who was going to grow
into a happy, healthy octogenarian

135
00:08:01,204 --> 00:08:02,413
and who wasn't.

136
00:08:03,163 --> 00:08:07,413
And when we gathered together
everything we knew about them

137
00:08:07,413 --> 00:08:08,788
at age 50,

138
00:08:09,579 --> 00:08:12,121
it wasn't their middle age
cholesterol levels

139
00:08:12,121 --> 00:08:15,038
that predicted how they
were going to grow old.

140
00:08:15,038 --> 00:08:18,496
It was how satisfied they were
in their relationships.

141
00:08:18,538 --> 00:08:23,413
The people who were the most satisfied
in their relationships at age 50
The people who were the most satisfied
in their relationships at age 50

142
00:08:23,454 --> 00:08:25,871
were the healthiest at age 80.

143
00:08:27,163 --> 00:08:30,371
And good, close relationships
seem to buffer us

144
00:08:30,371 --> 00:08:33,121
from some of the slings and arrows
of getting old.

145
00:08:33,996 --> 00:08:37,954
Our most happily partnered men and women

146
00:08:37,996 --> 00:08:40,037
reported, in their 80s,

147
00:08:40,037 --> 00:08:42,996
that on the days
when they had more physical pain,

148
00:08:42,996 --> 00:08:44,996
their mood stayed just as happy.

149
00:08:45,912 --> 00:08:49,162
But the people who were
in unhappy relationships,

150
00:08:49,162 --> 00:08:52,121
on the days when they
reported more physical pain,
on the days when they
reported more physical pain,

151
00:08:52,121 --> 00:08:55,162
it was magnified by more emotional pain.

152
00:08:55,871 --> 00:09:00,246
And the third big lesson that we learned
about relationships and our health

153
00:09:00,246 --> 00:09:03,496
is that good relationships
don't just protect our bodies,

154
00:09:03,537 --> 00:09:04,996
they protect our brains.

155
00:09:05,954 --> 00:09:10,579
It turns out that being
in a securely attached relationship

156
00:09:10,621 --> 00:09:14,496
to another person in your 80s
is protective,

157
00:09:14,537 --> 00:09:16,496
that the people who are in relationships

158
00:09:16,537 --> 00:09:20,662
where they really feel they can count
on the other person in times of need,

159
00:09:20,704 --> 00:09:24,412
those people's memories
stay sharper longer.
those people's memories
stay sharper longer.

160
00:09:24,412 --> 00:09:25,912
And the people in relationships

161
00:09:25,954 --> 00:09:29,079
where they feel they really
can't count on the other one,

162
00:09:29,079 --> 00:09:32,996
those are the people who experience
earlier memory decline.

163
00:09:33,996 --> 00:09:37,454
And those good relationships,
they don't have to be smooth all the time.

164
00:09:37,496 --> 00:09:41,079
Some of our octogenarian couples
could bicker with each other

165
00:09:41,079 --> 00:09:42,829
day in and day out,

166
00:09:42,871 --> 00:09:46,037
but as long as they felt that they
could really count on the other

167
00:09:46,037 --> 00:09:47,871
when the going got tough,

168
00:09:53,079 --> 00:09:55,829
those arguments didn't take a toll
on their memories.
So this message,

169
00:09:55,871 --> 00:10:01,537
that good, close relationships
are good for our health and well-being,

170
00:10:01,579 --> 00:10:04,496
this is wisdom that's as old as the hills.

171
00:10:04,537 --> 00:10:08,371
Why is this so hard to get
and so easy to ignore?

172
00:10:09,037 --> 00:10:10,496
Well, we're human.

173
00:10:10,537 --> 00:10:13,371
What we'd really like is a quick fix,

174
00:10:13,371 --> 00:10:15,079
something we can get

175
00:10:15,079 --> 00:10:17,871
that'll make our lives good
and keep them that way.

176
00:10:18,829 --> 00:10:22,162
Relationships are messy
and they're complicated

177
00:10:22,162 --> 00:10:25,996
and the hard work of tending
to family and friends,
and the hard work of tending
to family and friends,

178
00:10:25,996 --> 00:10:28,662
it's not sexy or glamorous.

179
00:10:28,704 --> 00:10:32,037
It's also lifelong. It never ends.

180
00:10:32,037 --> 00:10:37,121
The people in our 75-year study
who were the happiest in retirement

181
00:10:37,121 --> 00:10:42,954
were the people who had actively worked
to replace workmates with new playmates.

182
00:10:42,996 --> 00:10:45,954
Just like the millennials
in that recent survey,

183
00:10:45,996 --> 00:10:49,579
many of our men when they
were starting out as young adults

184
00:10:49,621 --> 00:10:53,621
really believed that fame and wealth
and high achievement

185
00:10:53,662 --> 00:10:57,579
were what they needed to go after
to have a good life.
were what they needed to go after
to have a good life.

186
00:10:57,621 --> 00:11:01,787
But over and over, over these 75 years,
our study has shown

187
00:11:01,829 --> 00:11:07,454
that the people who fared the best were
the people who leaned in to relationships,

188
00:11:07,496 --> 00:11:10,746
with family, with friends, with community.

189
00:11:12,579 --> 00:11:14,537
So what about you?

190
00:11:14,579 --> 00:11:18,329
Let's say you're 25,
or you're 40, or you're 60.

191
00:11:19,287 --> 00:11:22,246
What might leaning in
to relationships even look like?

192
00:11:23,246 --> 00:11:26,371
Well, the possibilities
are practically endless.
Well, the possibilities
are practically endless.

193
00:11:27,079 --> 00:11:33,204
It might be something as simple
as replacing screen time with people time

194
00:11:33,204 --> 00:11:37,662
or livening up a stale relationship
by doing something new together,

195
00:11:37,704 --> 00:11:39,912
long walks or date nights,

196
00:11:40,871 --> 00:11:45,704
or reaching out to that family member
who you haven't spoken to in years,

197
00:11:45,746 --> 00:11:49,246
because those all-too-common family feuds

198
00:11:49,246 --> 00:11:51,454
take a terrible toll

199
00:11:51,496 --> 00:11:53,579
on the people who hold the grudges.

200
00:11:55,496 --> 00:11:59,412
I'd like to close with a quote
from Mark Twain.
I'd like to close with a quote
from Mark Twain.

201
00:12:00,787 --> 00:12:03,162
More than a century ago,

202
00:12:03,162 --> 00:12:05,787
he was looking back on his life,

203
00:12:05,829 --> 00:12:07,079
and he wrote this:

204
00:12:08,329 --> 00:12:12,037
"There isn't time, so brief is life,

205
00:12:12,037 --> 00:12:17,204
for bickerings, apologies,
heartburnings, callings to account.

206
00:12:18,204 --> 00:12:21,037
There is only time for loving,

207
00:12:21,037 --> 00:12:24,787
and but an instant,
so to speak, for that."

208
00:12:26,246 --> 00:12:30,621
The good life is built
with good relationships.
The good life is built
with good relationships.

209
00:12:30,662 --> 00:12:31,871
Thank you.

210
00:12:31,912 --> 00:12:37,329
(Applause)
